    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="ly-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="ly-what" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">lymphoma?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your lymphatic system is part of your immune system. It includes the lymph nodes (glands), spleen, thymus, tonsils and bone marrow, connected by thin tubes that carry a fluid called lymph. It helps your body fight infections.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Lymphoma starts when lymphocytes (B cells, T cells or NK cells) change and grow out of control. These abnormal cells collect in the lymph nodes and other organs, causing swelling. Lymphoma can also start in, or spread to, organs outside the lymph nodes, such as the stomach, skin, brain or bone marrow.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">The right lymphoma treatment in Bangalore starts with knowing the exact type. Dr. Neema Bhat, a US-trained hematologist and bone marrow transplant specialist with 10+ years of experience, treats all types of lymphoma in adults and children.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg>The two main groups</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Hodgkin lymphoma:</b> has a special abnormal cell called the Reed-Sternberg cell. It is common in young adults and has very high cure rates.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL):</b> a large group of more than 60 subtypes. Some grow slowly (indolent), while others grow fast (aggressive) but often respond well to treatment.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">About 55,300 people in India were diagnosed with lymphoma in 2022: about 11,700 with Hodgkin lymphoma and 43,600 with non-Hodgkin lymphoma (National Cancer Registry Programme, ICMR). Swollen glands are often mistaken for an infection or tuberculosis. If a lump does not go away, timely lymphoma treatment in Bangalore can make a big difference.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="ly-sym">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs &amp; symptoms</p><h2 class="title" id="ly-sym" data-fx="wipe">Common signs of <span class="grad-text">lymphoma</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">The most common sign is a painless swelling of the lymph nodes. Watch for:</p>
        </div><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Painless lump</b><span>In the neck, armpit or groin that does not go away after 2 to 3 weeks</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><b>Recurring fever</b><span>Fever that keeps coming back without an infection</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-moon"/></svg><b>Night sweats</b><span>Heavy sweats that soak your clothes or bedsheets</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Weight loss</b><span>Losing weight without trying</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Tiredness</b><span>Constant tiredness or weakness</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><b>Itching</b><span>Itching all over the body</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-lungs"/></svg><b>Cough or breathlessness</b><span>Cough or chest pain, from swollen glands in the chest</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Swollen tummy</b><span>Or pain in the upper left side, from an enlarged spleen</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><b>Poor appetite</b><span>Loss of appetite or feeling full quickly</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><b>Frequent infections</b><span>Infections that keep coming back</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Fever, drenching night sweats and weight loss of more than 10% in 6 months are called B symptoms. They help doctors stage the lymphoma and plan treatment.</p><div class="cols2 mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Lymphoma symptoms in children</h3><p class="box__sub">Children may have swollen glands in the neck, a swollen tummy, cough or breathing difficulty, fever or tiredness. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatric hematologist-oncologist, so children with lymphoma get specialised care.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg>See a doctor urgently</h3><p class="box__sub">If you have sudden breathlessness, swelling of the face, neck or arms, severe tummy pain, weakness in the legs, or high fever with chills.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="ly-cause">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="ly-cause"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>What causes lymphoma and who is at risk?</h3><p class="box__sub">In most people, the exact cause is not known. It starts with changes (mutations) in the DNA of a lymphocyte. Lymphoma is not contagious. The risk is higher for:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Age:</b> Hodgkin lymphoma is more common in young adults (15 to 35) and in people over 55. Most non-Hodgkin lymphomas are more common after 60.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Men, who get lymphoma slightly more often than women</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Weak immune system:</b> HIV infection, organ transplant patients, or people taking long-term immune-suppressing medicines</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Autoimmune diseases:</b> such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, Sjogren’s syndrome and celiac disease</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Certain infections:</b> Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), Helicobacter pylori (stomach), hepatitis B and C, and HTLV-1</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Family history:</b> a parent, brother or sister with lymphoma</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Chemical exposure:</b> long-term exposure to some pesticides, herbicides and solvents</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Previous cancer treatment with chemotherapy or radiation</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Most people with these risk factors never get lymphoma, and many people with lymphoma have no known risk factor.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>What happens if lymphoma is left untreated?</h3><p class="box__sub">Aggressive lymphomas can grow within weeks, and even slow-growing types can change over time. Without treatment, lymphoma can cause:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Spread to other organs:</b> such as the liver, lungs, bone marrow, brain or spinal cord.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Pressure on vital structures:</b> swollen glands in the chest can press on the windpipe or a large vein (superior vena cava syndrome), causing breathlessness and face swelling.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Bone marrow failure:</b> low blood counts lead to anemia, infections and bleeding.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Serious infections because the immune system is weakened.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Kidney problems:</b> from blocked urine flow or from fast-growing lymphoma cells breaking down (tumour lysis).</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Fewer treatment choices and a lower chance of cure.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">The good news: most lymphomas respond well to treatment, and many people are cured when treatment starts early.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="ly-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Types</p><h2 class="title" id="ly-types" data-fx="wipe">Types of lymphoma <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Type of lymphoma</th><th>What it means in simple words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Classical Hodgkin lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The most common type of Hodgkin lymphoma. Often affects young adults and is usually curable.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A rare, slow-growing type of Hodgkin lymphoma.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The most common non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Grows fast but is often curable.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Follicular lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A common slow-growing lymphoma. Can often be watched or treated gently for years.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Mantle cell lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A less common type that can be slow or fast-growing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Marginal zone and MALT lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Slow-growing types. MALT lymphoma of the stomach is often linked to H. pylori infection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Burkitt lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A very fast-growing type, more common in children and young adults. Needs urgent treatment and is often curable.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The lymph node form of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). Usually slow-growing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>T-cell and NK-cell lymphomas</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Less common types, including peripheral T-cell, anaplastic large cell and NK/T-cell lymphoma.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Lymphoblastic lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A fast-growing type, mostly in children and teenagers, treated like leukemia.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Primary CNS lymphoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Lymphoma that starts in the brain, spinal cord or eyes.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Type of lymphoma"><b>Lymphoma in children</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Specialised care for Hodgkin, Burkitt, lymphoblastic and anaplastic large cell lymphoma in children.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="split split--top mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg>Stages of lymphoma</h3><p class="box__sub">Doctors use the Lugano staging system to stage most lymphomas, based on where the lymphoma is found. Stage I and II are called early (limited) stage, and Stage III and IV are called advanced stage. Even advanced-stage lymphoma is often curable.</p></div>
          <div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage I</b></td><td data-label="What it means">One lymph node area or one organ is affected.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage II</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Two or more lymph node areas on the same side of the diaphragm (the muscle under the lungs).</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage III</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Lymph node areas on both sides of the diaphragm, or nodes above the diaphragm with the spleen.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage IV</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Lymphoma has spread outside the lymph nodes, such as to the liver, lungs or bone marrow.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="ly-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="ly-dx" data-fx="wipe">How is lymphoma <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">A biopsy is the only way to confirm lymphoma and find its exact type. Dr. Neema Bhat may advise:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Physical examination</b><p>Checks the lymph nodes, liver and spleen.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Lymph node biopsy</b><p>The whole node (excisional) or a core needle sample is removed and examined. A fine needle aspiration (FNAC) alone is usually not enough.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Immunohistochemistry</b><p>Special stains on the biopsy identify the exact type of lymphoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Flow cytometry</b><p>Studies the cells in blood, bone marrow or fluid samples.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Cytogenetics, FISH &amp; molecular tests</b><p>Find genetic changes that help classify the lymphoma and guide treatment.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Blood tests</b><p>CBC, kidney and liver function, LDH, uric acid and beta-2 microglobulin.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Viral tests</b><p>Hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV, which matter before treatment.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>PET-CT scan</b><p>Shows where the lymphoma is in the body and is used for staging and checking response.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>CT or MRI scan</b><p>Used in some cases, including for the brain and spine.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">10</span><div><b>Bone marrow biopsy</b><p>In selected cases, to check if lymphoma has reached the bone marrow.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">11</span><div><b>Lumbar puncture</b><p>In some fast-growing lymphomas, to check the fluid around the brain and spine.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">12</span><div><b>Heart and lung tests</b><p>An echocardiogram and lung function tests before treatment.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="ly-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="ly-tx" data-fx="wipe">Lymphoma treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Treatment depends on the type of lymphoma, its stage, how fast it is growing, your age and your overall health. Some slow-growing lymphomas need no treatment at first, while fast-growing types need treatment straight away.</p>
        </div><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Watch and wait</h3><p>Active monitoring for some slow-growing lymphomas with no symptoms, with regular check-ups and scans.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Chemotherapy</h3><p>Anti-cancer medicines given in planned cycles, usually as a combination. Most cycles are given as daycare treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Chemoimmunotherapy</h3><p>Chemotherapy given together with a monoclonal antibody, the standard first treatment for most B-cell lymphomas.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg><h3>Monoclonal antibody therapy</h3><p>Antibodies that attach to markers on lymphoma cells, such as CD20. Also used as maintenance in some slow-growing lymphomas.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Targeted therapy</h3><p>Medicines, often tablets, that block signals lymphoma cells need to grow, such as BTK inhibitors.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Antibody-drug conjugates</h3><p>Antibodies that carry an anti-cancer medicine directly to lymphoma cells.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Immune checkpoint inhibitors</h3><p>Immunotherapy that helps the immune system attack lymphoma cells, mainly for relapsed Hodgkin lymphoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-link"/></svg><h3>Bispecific antibodies</h3><p>Newer immunotherapy that links immune cells to lymphoma cells, for relapsed lymphomas, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><h3>CAR-T cell therapy</h3><p>The patient’s own immune cells are modified to attack lymphoma, for selected relapsed or refractory lymphomas.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Radiation therapy</h3><p>Targeted radiation to affected lymph node areas, alone in early-stage disease or after chemotherapy.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Autologous stem cell transplant</h3><p>High-dose chemotherapy followed by return of your own stem cells, for lymphoma that relapses or is high-risk. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Allogeneic (donor) stem cell transplant</h3><p>For selected patients with relapsed or aggressive lymphoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><h3>Treating the infection behind it</h3><p>For example, antibiotics for H. pylori in stomach MALT lymphoma, or antiviral treatment in hepatitis C-related lymphoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg><h3>CNS prophylaxis</h3><p>Treatment to prevent lymphoma from spreading to the brain in high-risk patients.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><h3>Supportive care</h3><p>Prevention of infections and tumour lysis, hepatitis B protection, count-boosting medicines and transfusions when needed.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><h3>Fertility preservation counselling</h3><p>Sperm or egg freezing before treatment for young patients.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Counselling &amp; palliative care</h3><p>Emotional support and symptom control for patients and families, at any stage of treatment.</p></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Lymphoma treatment works best when the full course is completed.</b> Do not stop or delay treatment because the swelling has gone down.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="ly-life">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="ly-life"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Living well during and after lymphoma treatment</h3><p class="box__sub">These steps help you stay strong and lower the risk of problems during treatment:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Check for fever:</b> a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or more during chemotherapy needs urgent medical attention.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent infections:</b> wash hands often, avoid crowds and sick people when counts are low, and take the vaccines your doctor advises. Live vaccines are usually avoided during treatment.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Eat safe, healthy food:</b> freshly cooked home food, enough protein (dal, eggs, paneer, curd, fish, chicken), fruits and boiled or filtered water.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Drink plenty of fluids, especially in the first days of chemotherapy, to protect the kidneys.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Stay gently active:</b> walking and light exercise reduce tiredness.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Look after your mouth and skin:</b> use a soft toothbrush, and protect skin treated with radiation from the sun.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Avoid smoking and alcohol, and check with your doctor before taking herbal or home remedies.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep every follow-up appointment. Check-ups catch any relapse early and look for late effects on the heart, lungs, thyroid and fertility.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Lymphoma cannot usually be prevented. But a swollen gland, fever or weight loss that does not go away should always be checked early.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

