        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Which doctor should I see for lymphoma in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A hematologist or hemato-oncologist (blood cancer specialist) treats lymphoma. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US-trained hematologist in Bangalore who treats all types of lymphoma in adults and children.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">Is lymphoma curable?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Many lymphomas are curable. Most people with Hodgkin lymphoma and many with aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphoma, such as DLBCL, are cured with treatment. Slow-growing lymphomas are usually not cured but can be controlled for many years.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">Does every swollen lymph node mean lymphoma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Most swollen glands are caused by infections and go away in 2 to 3 weeks. A painless lump that stays, grows, or comes with fever, night sweats or weight loss should be checked by a doctor.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">How is lymphoma different from TB of the lymph nodes?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Both can cause swollen glands, fever and weight loss, so they are often confused in India. A proper biopsy tells them apart. Never start TB treatment without a confirmed diagnosis.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">What is the difference between Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Hodgkin lymphoma has a special cell called the Reed-Sternberg cell and usually spreads in an orderly way from one lymph node group to the next. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a larger group with many subtypes that behave differently.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">How long does lymphoma treatment take?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Most treatment is given in cycles every 2 to 3 weeks, for about 3 to 6 months. Some slow-growing lymphomas need maintenance therapy for longer, while others need only watching.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">Is chemotherapy the only treatment for lymphoma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Many patients receive chemotherapy combined with immunotherapy. Radiation, targeted therapy, stem cell transplant and CAR-T cell therapy are also used, depending on the type and stage.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">Can lymphoma come back after treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes, it can, especially in slow-growing types. Regular follow-up helps find it early. If it returns, many effective treatments, including stem cell transplant, are available.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">Can children get lymphoma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes. Hodgkin, Burkitt and lymphoblastic lymphomas are seen in children and teenagers, and most children are cured with treatment. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatric hematologist-oncologist.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">How much does lymphoma treatment cost in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The cost depends on the type of lymphoma, the medicines used, the number of cycles, and whether radiation or a transplant is needed. The clinic can share an estimate after your first consultation.</p></details></div>
